|> Exercise
    handle=_spherical_to_cartesian_coordinates_2_

    If, 
    in Exercise >>_spherical_to_cartesian_coordinates_1_,
    we modify the definition of the angle $\phi$
    to be $P$'s elevation above the $xy$-plane,
    $\phi \in [-\eta, \eta]$,
    as illustrated below, 
    how are the corresponding change-of-coordinate 
    formulas affected?

    |> Image
        src=tmp-images/svg_ch5_ex_spherical_coordinates_otherway_statement.svg

    |> Solution

        Since
        $$
        \phi \in [-\eta, \eta]
        $$
        one has
        $$
        \cos(\phi) \geq 0
        $$
        and
        $$
        R\cos(\phi) \geq 0
        $$
        from which $R\cos(\phi)$ becomes the 
        length of
        $P$'s projection onto the $xy$-plane,
        similarly to $R\sin(\phi)$ being that
        length in Exercise >>_spherical_to_cartesian_coordinates_1_,
        while on the other the other hand,
        $$
        R\sin(\phi)
        $$
        is a signed quantity equal to $z$, similarly
        to $R\cos(\phi)$ in Exercise >>_spherical_to_cartesian_coordinates_1_:

        |> Image
            src=tmp-images/svg_ch5_ex_spherical_coordinates_otherway_Rcos_phi.svg

        The coordinate transformations are
        thus obtained by interchanging “$\sin(\phi)$” and
        “$\cos(\phi)$” in the formulas of Exercise >>_spherical_to_cartesian_coordinates_1_,
        giving us:
        !! The coordinate transformation is thus...

        |> Boxed
            style=margin-bottom:0em;
            $$
            x = R\cos(\phi)\cos(\theta)
            $$

            $$
            y = R\cos(\phi)\sin(\theta)
            $$

            $$
            z = R\sin(\phi)
            $$

        |> StarDivider